            <ArticleTitle>Regulatory T cells use &quot;Itch&quot; to control asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>4576-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1172/JCI72477</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">72477</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Regulatory T cells (Tregs) control type 2 T helper cell-mediated (Th2-mediated) lung inflammation, but the molecular mechanisms by which Tregs execute this activity remain elusive. In this issue of the JCI, Jin et al. reveal that Itch, a HECT-type E3 ubiquitin ligase in Tregs, plays a specific role in restraining Th2 cell responses. This finding has important implications for understanding the pathogenesis of allergy and asthma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
